   "use": "Said every day, before meals and on coming in: te aratte kite (go wash your hands), te aratta? O often drops in these reminders.",
   "notes": "Only literal. Ashi o arau (wash your feet) is the idiom for quitting a shady line of work; pulling out of a deal or matter is te o hiku.",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "gohan da yo. te aratte kite.",
    "ja": "ごはんだよ。手洗ってきて。",
    "english": "Dinner's ready. Go wash your hands."
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "gohan desu yo. te o aratte kite kudasai.",
    "ja": "ごはんですよ。手を洗ってきてください。",
    "english": "The meal's ready. Please go and wash your hands."
   }
  },
