   "englishVerb": "get",
   "trap": "English 'get a haircut' suggests kitte morau or the dictionary word sanpatsu suru (old-fashioned). Kitte morau works, but natives just say kami kitta (or katto shita), even when a stylist did it.",
   "partners": [
    "maegami (bangs)",
    "yasai (vegetables)"
   ],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "Mostly heard in the past tense with o dropped: kami kitta? (did you get a haircut?), a stock small-talk line. For the salon trip itself, say kami kiri ni iku (go get a haircut).",
   "notes": "Don't mix it up with kiru 'wear' (fuku o kiru): cut is kitta / kirimasu, wear is kita / kimasu. By ear, cut drops after ki (KI-tta) and wear is flat (kita). Kami (hair) sounds the same as kami 'paper' (10-list-nouns #14), so kami o kiru can also mean cut paper. Context decides, and kami no ke makes 'hair' explicit.",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "are, kami kitta?",
    "ja": "あれ、髪切った？",
    "english": "Oh, did you get a haircut?"
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "are, kami kirimashita?",
    "ja": "あれ、髪切りました？",
    "english": "Oh, did you get your hair cut?"
   }
  },
